1. **A method for producing organic pigment microparticles**, the method comprising:  
   providing a processing apparatus comprising a first processing surface and a second processing surface disposed in opposed relation, at least one of the first and second processing surfaces being movable toward and away from the other and rotatable relative to the other;  
   rotating at least one of the processing surfaces to form between the processing surfaces a thin processing gap having a variable spacing;  
   supplying a first fluid comprising an organic pigment dissolved or dispersed in a first liquid medium to the processing gap;  
   supplying a second fluid comprising a precipitation-inducing liquid medium to the processing gap, the second fluid being capable of reducing the solubility of the organic pigment, changing a pH, causing a pigment-forming reaction, or a combination thereof;  
   mixing the first fluid and the second fluid within a thin-film region formed between the opposed processing surfaces under shear generated by the relative rotation;  
   causing nucleation or comminution of the organic pigment in the thin-film region to form organic pigment microparticles;  
   discharging a fluid containing the organic pigment microparticles from a peripheral region of the processing gap; and  
   recovering the organic pigment microparticles from the discharged fluid.

2. **The method of claim 1**, wherein fluid pressure within the processing gap urges the movable processing surface away from the opposed processing surface, and a biasing force urges the movable processing surface toward the opposed processing surface, thereby self-adjusting the spacing of the processing gap.

3. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the first and second fluids are introduced through separate flow passages and initially contact one another within the processing gap.

4. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the organic pigment is selected from azo pigments, phthalocyanine pigments, quinacridone pigments, perylene pigments, perinone pigments, diketopyrrolopyrrole pigments, anthraquinone pigments, indigoid pigments, and mixtures thereof.

5. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the precipitation-inducing liquid medium comprises an antisolvent in which the organic pigment has lower solubility than in the first liquid medium.

6. **The method of claim 1**, wherein formation of the organic pigment microparticles comprises at least one of solvent-shift precipitation, acid-base precipitation, chemical synthesis, crystal-form conversion, and shear-assisted size reduction.

7. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising controlling at least one of rotational speed, fluid pressure, fluid flow rate, processing temperature, processing-gap spacing, pigment concentration, solvent composition, or residence time to obtain a selected particle-size distribution.

8. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the resulting organic pigment microparticles have a volume-based median particle diameter of not greater than 10 µm.

9. **The method of claim 8**, wherein the resulting organic pigment microparticles have a volume-based median particle diameter of from 10 nm to 1 µm.

10. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising adding a dispersant, surfactant, resin, crystal-growth inhibitor, or surface-treatment agent to at least one of the first fluid and the second fluid to inhibit agglomeration or modify a surface property of the microparticles.

11. **The method of claim 1**, further comprising cooling, washing, filtering, centrifuging, drying, or combinations thereof after discharging the fluid containing the organic pigment microparticles.

12. **The method of claim 1**, wherein the shear and rapid mixing within the thin-film region produce the organic pigment microparticles with a narrower particle-size distribution than is obtained by batchwise mixing of the first and second fluids under otherwise corresponding formulation conditions.
